-- Ring 1: Attention as a Krein-Space Metric Evaluator
---------------------------------------------------------------

/- A Krein space is a vector space equipped with an indefinite inner
   product. Unlike a Hilbert space (which has a positive-definite metric),
   a Krein space allows timelike (negative norm), spacelike (positive norm),
   and lightlike (null norm) vectors.

   The Minkowski metric η = diag(1,−1,−1,−1) defines a Krein structure
   on ℝ⁴. The attention mechanism in a Transformer computes:
     Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(Q·K†/√d)·V

   where Q·K† is an inner product between query and key vectors.
   If this inner product is chosen to resemble the Minkowski (Krein)
   metric, attention scores can be described with causal terminology:
   - Timelike pairs: strong attention (causally connected tokens)
   - Spacelike pairs: weak/zero attention (causally disconnected)
   - Lightlike pairs: edge attention (maximum context window)

   The softmax then normalizes these causal weights into a probability
   distribution. This is a modeling analogy, not a claim of physical
   Lorentz invariance for a Transformer. -/

def attentionKreinMetric (Q K : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) : ℂ :=
  -- The Krein inner product = trace of the product in the chiral basis
  -- Q·K† in the Minkowski metric = the 2×2 Hermitian trace
  Matrix.trace (Q * Matrix.conjTranspose K)

theorem attentionKreinMetric_zero_left (K : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) :
  attentionKreinMetric 0 K = 0 := by
  simp [attentionKreinMetric]



