-- Ring 3: Positional Encoding — Mellin Transforms and Conformal Scale
---------------------------------------------------------------

/- The Mellin transform:
   M[f](s) = ∫_0^∞ f(x) x^{s-1} dx

   Unlike the Fourier transform (which maps translation to phase),
   the Mellin transform maps scaling x → λx to multiplication by λ^{-s}.
   This is the natural transform for SCALE-INVARIANT information processing.

   In the LLM context, replacing Fourier (RoPE) with Mellin positional
   encoding means the model processes information at all scales
   simultaneously. A token at position n is encoded by its log-scale:
     n → exp(i·θ·ln n)

   This splits the representation into three geometric regimes:
   - Elliptic:   compressive scaling (zooming IN)
   - Parabolic:  flat interface / monodromy (the critical strip)
   - Hyperbolic: expansive scaling (zooming OUT, AdS bulk)

   The critical strip Re(s) ∈ (0,1) also appears in the study of the
   Riemann ζ-function. The analogy here is about shared scale language,
   not a claim that positional encoding evaluates ζ. -/

def mellinEncoding (n : ℕ) (θ : ℝ) : ℂ :=
  Complex.exp (Complex.I * (θ : ℂ) * Real.log (n : ℝ))


